---
const { code, oldCode = "" } = Astro.props;
---

<h2>How to find this code on your tire</h2>
<p>
  The plant code is the first group of the tire identification number molded into the sidewall.
  The letters DOT in front of it are a certification mark, not the plant code.
  The symbol rules are in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-574/section-574.5">49 CFR 574.5</a>.
</p>
<p>
  On a current new tire the number has 13 symbols: plant code <span class="code">{code}</span>, then six symbols the manufacturer assigns, then four digits for the week and the year.
  A retreaded tire's number in 49 CFR 574.5(a)(2) is 7 symbols: the plant code and the four-digit date.
  {
    oldCode ? (
      <>
        {" "}This record also lists former code <span class="code">{oldCode}</span>. Tires made under the older two-symbol layout can still show that code.
      </>
    ) : (
      " This record does not list a former two-symbol code."
    )
  }
</p>
<p>
  49 CFR 574.5 requires the full number on one sidewall. On many radial tires the other sidewall leaves the date off, so the plant code can sit on either side.
  If you do not see <span class="code">{code}</span> on the side you can read, check the other sidewall.
</p>
